null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"figure\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1093, 408, 1464, 585], \"page_id\": 77}], \"content\": \"In the first nine months of 2001, U.S. banking liabilities increased $7 billion, following a $92 billion increase last year. In both years, the increases were due to increases opposite the Caribbean financial centers.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1087, 610, 1470, 915], \"page_id\": 77}], \"content\": \"U.S. banking liabilities to foreigners, excluding long-term securities, are concentrated in international financial centers. The data on this page show that nearly one-half of U.S. banking liabilities currently is reported opposite the United Kingdom and the banking centers in the Caribbean. Foreigners domiciled in the rest of Europe and in Asia hold an additional 40 percent.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1091, 933, 1466, 1186], \"page_id\": 77}], \"content\": \"U.S. banking liabilities in the mid-1990s went through a growth spurt. The annual growth rate between 1993 and 1997 averaged 10 percent. From 1998 to 2000, growth slowed to about 5 percent per year, more in line with the 1989 through 1992 period. This year, growth has slowed considerably.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [138, 1214, 1056, 1888], \"page_id\": 77}], \"content\": null, \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"figure\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [144, 111, 173, 137], \"page_id\": 78}], \"content\": \"78\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [668, 111, 962, 137], \"page_id\": 78}], \"content\": \"CAPITAL MOVEMENTS\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [199, 182, 1432, 219], \"page_id\": 78}], \"content\": \"SECTION II.\\u2014Claims on Foreigners Reported by Banks in the United States\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \